{"sequence": 4, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:51:39", "event_type": "user_input", "payload": {"turn_id": "299c5a80-5f26-409f-a156-6309f49bb468", "prompt": "Explica la intuición geométrica y matricial detrás de la Transformada de Fourier Discreta (DFT). Luego, escribe un script en Python utilizando exclusivamente numpy (sin funciones de FFT preconstruidas) que implemente la DFT unidimensional optimizada mediante matrices de rotación complejas, e incluye un caso de prueba práctico con una señal senoidal compuesta."}}
{"sequence": 5, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:51:40", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "299c5a80-5f26-409f-a156-6309f49bb468", "phase": "routed", "path": "slow_path", "model": "qwen2.5:7b"}}
{"sequence": 6, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:52:04", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "299c5a80-5f26-409f-a156-6309f49bb468", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 7, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:52:04", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "299c5a80-5f26-409f-a156-6309f49bb468", "response": "La transformada discreta deFourier (DFT) puede entenderse intuitiva-mente a través de proyecciones ortogonales sobre bases exponenciales imaginarias. Matricialmente hablando, la matriz correspondiente tiene elementos $W_{nm}=exp(-j*2*pi*n*m/N)$ donde N son los puntos totales y n,m van desde 0 hasta N-1. Cada columna o fila representa una base unitaria y las operaciones involucradas pueden interpretarse visualizativamnete como mapear señales al espacio frecuencia usando estas bases complex-exponential. \n\nPara implementarlo sin usar métodos pre-construídos:\n\n```python \nimport numpy as np\n\ndef dft(x):\n    # x is the input signal of length NxN matrix where each row represents time domain data points\n    X=np.zeros_like(x,dtype=complex)\n    \n    for i in range(len(X)):\n        W=np.exp((-1.j * 2*np.pi*i)/len(X))\n        \n        for j,k in enumerate(range(i,x.shape[i]*x.shape[j],i+1)):            \n            if k == len(X): break\n                \n            X[:,k] += w**((j-i)%X.shape[0])*(x[k//i])\n                \n    return X\n```\n\nEste código define `dft` para procesamiento matricial vectorizado de señales bidimensionales pero modificado por bloques linealmente independientes aplicándolas iteradamente. Un test real podría usarse así:\n \n``` python   \nt_signal=sig.sin(4*x)+sig.cos(sig.logspace(.5,np.log(np.size(x)),np.size(t)))\nplt.plot(abs(dft(t_signal)))# muestra amplitud espectral normalizada\nplt.show()\n```\nEs importante notar que este método numéricamente intenso solo sirve para exposición didáctica/prueba; prácticamente FFT mejoraría muchísimo computacionalmente aunque aquí vemos estructura concepto implícita más claramente.", "outcome": "slow_path"}}
{"sequence": 8, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:53:14", "event_type": "user_input", "payload": {"turn_id": "f1aaae6a-dc5c-48a2-9a8f-4c821fea4e7a", "prompt": "Explain the geometric and matrix intuition behind the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT). Then, write a Python script using exclusively numpy (without pre-built FFT functions) that implements the one-dimensional DFT optimized via complex rotation matrices, and include a practical test case with a composite sinusoidal signal."}}
{"sequence": 9, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:53:14", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "f1aaae6a-dc5c-48a2-9a8f-4c821fea4e7a", "phase": "routed", "path": "slow_path", "model": "qwen2.5:7b"}}
{"sequence": 10, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:53:41", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "f1aaae6a-dc5c-48a2-9a8f-4c821fea4e7a", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 11, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:53:42", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "f1aaae6a-dc5c-48a2-9a8f-4c821fea4e7a", "response": "The discrete Fourier transform (DFT) can be understood through both its geometrical interpretation and by representing transformations with matrix operations involving rotations in multidimensional space.\n\nGeometrically, each sample point \\( x_n \\) represents coordinates on a unit circle where points move along this circumference at fixed intervals corresponding to different frequencies. The transformation decomposes these samples into their constituent circular motions—each component having constant magnitude but varying phase angles related specifically to frequency content within the original sequence.\n\nMatrix representation captures similar information more compactly utilizing orthogonal basis vectors derived from eigenvectors associated purely imaginary numbers under multiplication operation which rotate them around origin maintaining uniform angular velocity proportional to individual eigenvalues' real parts thus capturing same essence of decomposition differently structured algebraically than manually plotted circles methodologically simplifying calculations particularly useful computationally speaking especially during recursive application across sequences length greater equal two facilitating efficient algorithms including fast fourier transforms FTTs although here we avoid those optimizations focusing strictly upon direct computation approach instead.\n\nNow let’s implement such an optimization indirectly yet efficiently via basic arithmetic manipulations leveraging NumPy without resorting to built-in Fast Fourier Transformation methods:\n\n```python\nimport numpy\n\ndef dft(x):\n    N = len(x)\n    X_k = [sum((xi * numpy.exp(-numpy.complex(0,-2*numpy.pi*k/n)*i/N)) for i, xi in enumerate(x))\n           for k in range(N)]\n    return X_k\n\n# Test Case - Composite Sinusoid Signal Example\nn_samples  = int(input('Enter number of time domain observations > '))\nfreq_1     , freq_2   = float(input('\\nEnter first harmonic frequency(frequency/nyquist)> ')), \\\n                        float(input(\"second harmonic frequency> \"))\n\nt          = np.linspace(start=0.,stop=n_samples-1,num=int(n_samples),endpoint=False,dtype=float)\n\nx         = lambda t : math.sin(freq_1*2*math.pi*t)+math.cos(freq_2*(4+3/(8**5))*2*math.pi*t)\nsignal_x   = list(map(lambda ti:x(ti),list(t)))\n\ntransformed_signal_y=dft(signal_x);\nprint(transformed_signal_y[:6]) # Outputting only initial six elements due large results array size typically far exceeding actual meaningfulness outside specific contexts like analysis validation etc...\n```\n\nThis script defines `dft` function taking input signal as parameter then returns transformed version using summations over exponential decaying terms whose arguments vary based off index positions inside loop allowing us essentially perform DFT computations element-wise while retaining full fidelity necessary even when dealing complex scenarios beyond simple single pure tones mixing multiple distinct harmonics together forming richer composite waveforms often found nature man-made alike phenomena making spectral analyses crucial tools widely employed scientific engineering domains today!", "outcome": "slow_path"}}
{"sequence": 12, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:54:54", "event_type": "user_input", "payload": {"turn_id": "aaadd30b-f5c0-45b9-a878-cc1f93a74819", "prompt": "Implement a thread-safe Least Recently Used (LRU) cache system in Python using threading.Lock and OrderedDict. Add a Time-To-Live (TTL) expiration mechanism for each key and a background cleanup method to prevent memory leaks. Include a concurrent usage example."}}
{"sequence": 13, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:54:54", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "aaadd30b-f5c0-45b9-a878-cc1f93a74819", "phase": "routed", "path": "slow_path", "model": "qwen2.5:7b"}}
{"sequence": 14, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:55:15", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "aaadd30b-f5c0-45b9-a878-cc1f93a74819", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 15, "timestamp": "2026-08-28T11:55:15", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "aaadd30b-f5c0-45b9-a878-cc1f93a74819", "response": "To implement an LRU Cache System with TTL Expiration Mechanism:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCacheWithTTLE(object):\n    def __init__(self, capacity=5, ttl_seconds=60):\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n        self.capacity = capacity \n        self.ttl = ttl_seconds \n\ndef get(self, key):  \n   item = self.cache.pop(key)\n   value, timestamp = item[:1] + item[-3:]\n   current_time = time.time()   \n   age_of_value_in_cache = int(current_time - timestamp)\n\n   # Check TTL before returning cached data    \n   return None  # Or handle timeout exception here according requirements\n   \nif 'age' > 'ttl':\n     raise TimeoutError(f\"Key {key} expired\")\n\nreturn value  \n\ndef set(self, key, value):\n\n# Cleanup logic goes after setting new entries     \nfor k,v in list(cache.items()):\n      now=time.time()-v['timestamp']\n      if v[\"time_to_live\"] <now :\n           del cache[k]\n\n# Inserting actual operation...\nvalue_with_timestamp={\"data\": value,\"timestamp\":int(time().time())}\ntry:self.get(key=key);del self.set[key];pass\nexcept KeyError:self.cache.update({key : [None]+list(value)+['new']})\n\n```\nThis code snippet outlines the basic structure of such a class `LRUCache`. It uses an ordered dictionary (`OrderedDict`) which keeps track based on access order but does require manual handling due limitations compared direct python caching libraries like functools.lru\\_cache . The implemented methods would need further refinement considering edge cases especially concurrency issues around updating timestamps etc., also ensure proper error catching throughout implementation while maintaining thread-safety through careful use lock objects where necessary via threading module constructs such as `.Lock()` used within critical sections ensuring atomic operations by synchronizing threads appropriately during shared resource modification timeslices / updates to prevent race conditions leading potential deadlocks otherwise uncontrolled chaos amongst multiple processes accessing same memory space concurrently thereby risking corrupt state machine resulting into undefined behavior/segfaults depending upon operating system scheduler policies governing process scheduling priorities among other factors influencing performance characteristics including latency bound guarantees needed for real-time systems particularly sensitive applications requiring hard limits imposed over jitter levels tolerated across network interfaces connecting client nodes towards server hosting backend services managing complex workflows involving large scale microservices architectures deployed using container technologies supporting DevOps CI pipelines.", "outcome": "slow_path"}}
