{"sequence": 138, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T08:37:34", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "48e8c353-3af5-44a4-8f11-90dc963a328c", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 139, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T08:37:34", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "48e8c353-3af5-44a4-8f11-90dc963a328c", "response": "Certainly! Here are some fundamental equations in mathematics:\n\n1. Pythagorean theorem: For a right triangle with legs \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), and hypotenuse \\(c\\), we have $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$.\n2. Euler's identity: $e^{i\\pi} + 1 = 0$, which connects five fundamental mathematical constants.\n3. Quadratic formula for solving \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\): $x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$.\n4. Area of a circle: $\\pi r^2$, where \\(r\\) is the radius.\n5. Volume of a sphere: $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, again using the radius \\(r\\).\n6. Newton's second law of motion: \\(F = ma\\), relating force to mass and acceleration.\n7. Binomial theorem: $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} {n \\choose k} a^{n-k}b^k$.\n8. Fundamental theorem of calculus: If \\(f\\) is continuous on \\([a, b]\\) and \\(g(x)\\) is an antiderivative of \\(f\\) on \\([a, b]\\), then $\\int_a^b f(x) dx = g(b) - g(a)$.\n\nThese equations are foundational in various branches of mathematics and have wide-ranging applications across science and engineering.", "outcome": "fast_path"}}
