{"sequence": 186, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:10:41", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "73f9f7b1-b85e-4ebc-9a0e-4273b0d20ebc", "phase": "routed", "path": "fast_path", "model": "qwen2.5:7b"}}
{"sequence": 187, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:11:00", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "73f9f7b1-b85e-4ebc-9a0e-4273b0d20ebc", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 188, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:11:00", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "73f9f7b1-b85e-4ebc-9a0e-4273b0d20ebc", "response": "The Einstein Field Equations with a cosmological constant are given by:\n\n\\[\nG_{\\mu\\nu} + \\Lambda g_{\\mu\\nu} = \\frac{8\\pi G}{c^4} T_{\\mu\\nu}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(G_{\\mu\\nu}\\) is the Einstein tensor representing the geometry of spacetime, defined through the Ricci curvature tensor (\\(R_{\\mu\\nu}\\)) and the metric tensor (\\(g_{\\mu\\nu}\\)) as follows:\n\n\\[ \nG_{\\mu\\nu} = R_{\\mu\\nu} - \\frac{1}{2} g_{\\mu\\nu} R\n\\]\n\nHere:\n- \\(R_{\\mu\\nu}\\) is the Ricci curvature tensor that describes how space-time curves due to mass and energy,\n- \\(R\\) is the scalar curvature obtained from contracting the Ricci tensor with the metric tensor: \\(R = g^{\\mu\\nu} R_{\\mu\\nu}\\),\n- \\(g_{\\mu\\nu}\\) is the metric tensor which defines the distance between two points in curved spacetime.\n\nThe term \\(\\Lambda g_{\\mu\\nu}\\) represents the contribution of the cosmological constant to the stress-energy-momentum tensor. \n\n\\(T_{\\mu\\nu}\\) denotes the stress-energy-momentum tensor which encapsulates information about matter distribution and flux density (energy flow):\n\n- The first index \\(\\mu\\) relates to the spatial component, while the second index \\(\\nu\\) corresponds to time.\n- Specifically, \\(T^{00}\\) contains the energy density at a point in spacetime,\n- \\(T^{i0}\\) ($i=1,2,3$) represent momentum flows along coordinate axes,\n- \\(T^{ij}$ for $i,j=1,2,3$ give shear stresses within an infinitesimal volume element perpendicular to those axes.\n\nThis equation states that the geometry of spacetime (\\(G_{\\mu\\nu}\\)) plus its vacuum energy content (\\(\\Lambda g_{\\mu\\nu}\\)), equates proportionally to the distribution of mass, energy, pressure, etc., as described by \\(T_{\\mu\\nu}\\).", "outcome": "fast_path"}}
