{"sequence": 195, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:12:06", "event_type": "turn_phase", "payload": {"turn_id": "b62d519f-92bf-4c49-89b6-5728e25b68a6", "phase": "generation_done", "stats": {}}}
{"sequence": 196, "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:12:06", "event_type": "response", "payload": {"turn_id": "b62d519f-92bf-4c49-89b6-5728e25b68a6", "response": "The time-independent Schrödinger equation for a one-dimensional (1D) quantum system can be derived from the general form of the Schrödinger equation:\n\n\\[ i\\hbar \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Psi(x,t) = \\hat{H}\\Psi(x,t) \\]\n\nwhere $\\hat{H}$ is the Hamiltonian operator. For an isolated particle with mass \\(m\\) moving under a potential \\(V(x)\\), the Hamiltonian in one dimension is given by\n\n\\[ \\hat{H} = -\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m} \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x^2} + V(x). \\]\n\nIn a 1D infinite potential well, we have \\(V(x) = 0\\) within the interval \\(0 < x < L\\), where \\(L\\) is the width of the well, and \\(V(x) = \\infty\\) outside this region, effectively confining the particle to the well.\n\nTo find stationary states, we assume that the wave function \\(\\Psi(x,t)\\) has the separation-of-variables form\n\n\\[ \\Psi(x,t) = \\psi(x)e^{-iEt/\\hbar}, \\]\n\nwhere \\(\\psi(x)\\) is independent of time and satisfies the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n\\[ -\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m} \\frac{d^2\\psi(x)}{dx^2} = E\\psi(x). \\]\n\nThe boundary conditions for \\(\\psi(x)\\) are determined by the nature of the problem:\n\n1. Since \\(V(x) = \\infty\\) outside the well (\\(x < 0\\) or \\(x > L\\)), the wave function must be zero at these points: \\(\\psi(0) = 0\\) and \\(\\psi(L) = 0\\).\n\nThese boundary conditions ensure that the probability density (which is \\(\\lvert\\psi(x)\\rvert^2\\)) vanishes outside the well, which is physically reasonable since a particle cannot exist in regions where its potential energy exceeds its total energy.", "outcome": "fast_path"}}
